BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 368 Td (  *what* you want \(LINQ: `.OrderBy\(\).GroupBy\(\).Select\(\)`\), not *how* to achieve it. DSA) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 357 Td (  requires you to dictate the exact byte-level, index-by-index execution mechanism.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 344 Td (* **The "Everything is an Object" Mental Model:** In C#, we get comfortable treating) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 333 Td (  everything as high-level heap allocations. We pass around `List<T>`, `IEnumerable<T>`, and) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 322 Td (  complex nested domain models. When an interviewer asks to manipulate an array in-place) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 311 Td (  without auxiliary memory, the developer struggles because they are conditioned to create) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 300 Td (  new collections with LINQ.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 287 Td (* **The Absence of Systematic Tracing:** In enterprise code, when something goes wrong, you) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 276 Td (  attach the Visual Studio debugger, inspect the locals window, and step through. In an) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 265 Td (  interview, **you are the debugger**. You must be able to maintain variable state inside) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 254 Td (  your head and walk through an execution table on a whiteboard or shared notepad.) Tj ET
