BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 541.0 Td (* **QuickSort with Median-of-3 Pivot:** Selects the median of the first, middle, and last) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 530.0 Td (  elements as the pivot. This eliminates the $O\(N^2\)$ worst-case on already-sorted or) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 519.0 Td (  reverse-sorted inputs.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 506.0 Td (* **HeapSort Fallback Guard:** If the recursion depth exceeds $2 \\times \\lfloor\\log_2) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 495.0 Td (  N\\rfloor$, IntroSort detects that QuickSort is degrading and immediately switches to) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 484.0 Td (  **HeapSort**. HeapSort guarantees an upper bound of $O\(N \\log N\)$ time and $O\(1\)$ space.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 471.0 Td (* **InsertionSort for Small Partitions:** When a sub-partition shrinks to $\\le 16$) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 460.0 Td (  elements, IntroSort switches to **InsertionSort**. On tiny arrays, InsertionSort) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 449.0 Td (  outperforms QuickSort because it has zero recursion overhead and exploits CPU L1 cache) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 438.0 Td (  locality with minimal branch mispredictions.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 45 425.0 Td ([NOTE] **Interview Answer:** *"Why doesn't .NET use MergeSort for `Array.Sort`?"*) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 45 409.0 Td ([NOTE] **Answer:** MergeSort requires $O\(N\)$ extra memory allocation to merge arrays.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 45 397.0 Td (IntroSort is an in-place sort requiring $O\(\\log N\)$ stack space with superior cache locality.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 45 385.0 Td (For reference types where stability is required, LINQ's `.OrderBy\(\)` uses an adaptive stable) Tj ET
