BT /F1 9 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 45 475.0 Td (#### Why CQRS?) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 45 459.0 Td ([NOTE] "Problem: Our read patterns \(reporting, dashboards\) were slowing down our write path) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 45 447.0 Td (because they shared the same complex domain model. Every report query was loading entire) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 45 435.0 Td (aggregates just to display a few fields.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 45 419.0 Td ([NOTE] Options: We considered query optimization with raw SQL views, or full CQRS with) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 45 407.0 Td (separate read models.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 45 391.0 Td ([NOTE] Decision: We chose CQRS because the performance issue was architectural - the read and) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 45 379.0 Td (write models fundamentally had different shapes. Read models can be flat and denormalized;) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 45 367.0 Td (write models need to enforce invariants.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 45 351.0 Td ([NOTE] Trade-off: More code. Every feature now has command handlers and query handlers. Junior) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 45 339.0 Td (developers found it harder to follow. We mitigated this with good folder structure and) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 45 327.0 Td (conventions.") Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 45 311.0 Td (#### Why Repository Pattern?) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 45 295.0 Td ([NOTE] "Problem: We wanted to abstract data access so our Application layer didn't depend on) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 45 283.0 Td (EF Core directly.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 45 267.0 Td ([NOTE] Options: Direct DbContext, Repository, Unit of Work.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 45 251.0 Td ([NOTE] Decision: Repository because it gives us a clean seam for unit testing - we can mock) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 45 239.0 Td (IOrderRepository without spinning up a real database.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 45 223.0 Td ([NOTE] Trade-off: We lose EF Core's rich querying capability at the application layer. Complex) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 45 211.0 Td (queries that would be one LINQ expression become custom methods on the repository. We accepted) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 45 199.0 Td (this because testability was a priority.") Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 45 183.0 Td (#### Why EF Core instead of Dapper?) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 45 167.0 Td ([NOTE] "Problem: We needed a data access layer that could handle complex domain model mapping) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 45 155.0 Td (and enforce business rules at the persistence layer.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 45 139.0 Td ([NOTE] Options: EF Core \(full ORM\), Dapper \(micro-ORM + raw SQL\), or ADO.NET.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 45 123.0 Td ([NOTE] Decision: EF Core for its Code-First migrations, change tracking, and the ability to) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 45 111.0 Td (map complex relationships. Dapper would require hand-writing SQL for every operation.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 45 95.0 Td ([NOTE] Trade-off: EF Core generates suboptimal SQL for complex queries. We added Dapper for) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 45 83.0 Td (reporting queries where we needed precise SQL control. So the final answer is: EF Core for) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 45 71.0 Td (writes and simple reads, Dapper for complex reporting queries.") Tj ET
