BT /F1 9 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 45 329.5 Td ([NOTE] "We had a bug in production where certain API requests would randomly return 500 errors) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 45 317.5 Td (about 0.5% of the time, with no pattern in the logs except `InvalidOperationException: A second) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 45 305.5 Td (operation was started on this context before a previous operation completed`.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 45 289.5 Td ([NOTE]) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 45 273.5 Td ([NOTE] This is the classic EF Core DbContext thread-safety violation. After analysis, I found) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 45 261.5 Td (a singleton service was injecting `DbContext`, which is scoped. When two concurrent requests) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 45 249.5 Td (hit the singleton, they shared the same DbContext instance, causing concurrent access.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 45 233.5 Td ([NOTE]) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 45 217.5 Td ([NOTE] The fix: change the singleton service to scoped, or better, inject) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 45 205.5 Td (`IDbContextFactory<AppDbContext>` and create a new context per operation.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 45 189.5 Td ([NOTE]) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 45 173.5 Td ([NOTE] Effect: Zero recurrence of that error. We also added a Roslyn analyzer rule to catch) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 45 161.5 Td (singleton-with-scoped-dependency anti-patterns at compile time.") Tj ET
