BT /F2 11.5 Tf 0.1 0.15 0.25 rg 45 463 Td (CATEGORY 1: C# BASIC & FUNDAMENTAL INTERNALS) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 45 445 Td (---) Tj ET
BT /F2 10 Tf 0.2 0.2 0.3 rg 45 429 Td (Q001: Value Types vs Reference Types Memory Allocation) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 414 Td (* **Expected Depth**: Intermediate) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 401 Td (* **Key Answer Points**:) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 388 Td (* Value types \(`struct`, `enum`, primitive numerics\) inherit directly from) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 377 Td (  `System.ValueType` \(which derives from `System.Object`\). Reference types \(`class`,) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 366 Td (  `interface`, `delegate`, `record class`, `string`, `object`\) inherit from `System.Object`.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 353 Td (* **Memory Location Myth Debunked**: Value types do *not* always live on the stack. Value) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 342 Td (  types are allocated inline wherever their container is declared: local variables of value) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 331 Td (  types inside methods reside on the Thread Stack; value type fields inside reference types) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 320 Td (  live on the Managed Heap inside the parent object's memory payload; value types captured) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 309 Td (  in closures \(lambdas, local functions\) or `async` state machines are lifted to) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 298 Td (  compiler-generated classes residing on the Heap.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 285 Td (* Reference types always consist of two parts: a reference/pointer \(4 bytes on 32-bit, 8) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 274 Td (  bytes on 64-bit architecture\) residing on the stack or enclosing object, and the instance) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 263 Td (  data allocated on the Managed Heap \(composed of Object Header / `SyncBlockIndex`,) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 252 Td (  `MethodTable` pointer, and instance fields\).) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 239 Td (* **The Trap**: Claiming "Value types always go on the stack, and reference types always go) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 228 Td (  on the heap." Interviewers will immediately ask what happens when a `struct` is a field of) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 217 Td (  a `class` or captured in a lambda.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 204 Td (* **Follow-up Question**: When a 64-bit runtime creates an instance of a class with an) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 193 Td (  empty body \(`class Empty {}`\), how many bytes are allocated on the heap? *\(Answer: 24) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 182 Td (  bytes minimum - 8 bytes SyncBlockIndex, 8 bytes MethodTable pointer, 8 bytes minimum) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 171 Td (  padding/alignment\).*) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 45 158 Td (---) Tj ET
BT /F2 10 Tf 0.2 0.2 0.3 rg 45 142 Td (Q002: Boxing and Unboxing Internal Execution Mechanics) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 127 Td (* **Expected Depth**: Intermediate) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 114 Td (* **Key Answer Points**:) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 101 Td (* **Boxing**: The implicit conversion of a value type to `object` or any interface it) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 90 Td (  implements. The CLR allocates a new object header \(SyncBlockIndex + MethodTable pointer\)) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 79 Td (  on the Small Object Heap \(SOH\), copies the raw byte-for-byte value payload into the newly) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 68 Td (  allocated heap memory, and returns a reference to this new heap object.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8 Tf 0.4 0.4 0.4 rg
