BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 780 Td (* **Unboxing**: An explicit two-step operation. First, the CLR checks that the object) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 769 Td (  reference is not null and points to a boxed instance of the exact target type \(throws) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 758 Td (  `InvalidCastException` if mismatched\). Second, it returns a managed interior pointer to) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 747 Td (  the value payload within the boxed instance. A subsequent IL instruction \(`ldobj`\) copies) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 736 Td (  the raw bytes from heap to the evaluation stack.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 723 Td (* **Performance Cost**: Boxing triggers heap allocations, increases GC Gen 0 pressure,) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 712 Td (  invalidates CPU cache locality, and adds CPU instruction overhead.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 699 Td (* **The Trap**: Believing that unboxing directly copies the value back to the stack.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 688 Td (  Unboxing only obtains the interior pointer; the subsequent IL `ldobj` instruction performs) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 677 Td (  the copy. Also, unboxing to an incompatible type \(e.g., boxing `int` and unboxing to) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 666 Td (  `long` or `short`\) fails at runtime with `InvalidCastException` even though an implicit) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 655 Td (  numeric conversion exists between them.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 642 Td (* **Follow-up Question**: Can you mutate the fields of a boxed struct without unboxing it) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 631 Td (  completely? *\(Answer: Yes, by casting the boxed object to an interface implemented by the) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 620 Td (  struct that contains mutating methods, or via unsafe reflection/interior pointers\).*) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 45 607 Td (---) Tj ET
BT /F2 10 Tf 0.2 0.2 0.3 rg 45 591 Td (Q003: String Immutability and the CLR String Interning Pool) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 576 Td (* **Expected Depth**: Intermediate) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 563 Td (* **Key Answer Points**:) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 550 Td (* `System.String` is a reference type with sealed, immutable semantics. Once instantiated) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 539 Td (  on the Managed Heap, its character buffer and length cannot be modified safely. Any) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 528 Td (  modification method \(`Replace`, `Substring`, `ToUpper`, concatenation `+`\) creates a) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 517 Td (  completely new string object on the heap.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 504 Td (* **CLR Interning**: To optimize memory, the CLR maintains an internal hash table called) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 493 Td (  the String Interning Pool \(AppDomain-scoped\). Literal strings in the IL metadata are) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 482 Td (  automatically interned by the runtime at assembly load time.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 469 Td (* Dynamic strings generated at runtime are *not* automatically interned. Developers can) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 458 Td (  manually query or register strings using `string.Intern\(str\)` and) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 447 Td (  `string.IsInterned\(str\)`.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 434 Td (* Comparing interned strings allows reference equality \(`ReferenceEquals\(s1, s2\)`\) running) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 423 Td (  in $O\(1\)$ pointer comparison rather than $O\(N\)$ character-by-character validation.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 410 Td (* **The Trap**: Using `string.Intern\(\)` indiscriminately in high-throughput enterprise) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 399 Td (  systems. Objects in the intern pool are rooted by the CLR and cannot be garbage collected) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 388 Td (  until the entire `AppDomain` unloads, leading to unbounded native memory leaks.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 375 Td (* **Follow-up Question**: What is the difference between `StringBuilder` capacity doubling) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 364 Td (  vs `string.Create\(\)` with `Span<char>` introduced in modern .NET? *\(Answer:) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 353 Td (  `StringBuilder` amortizes allocations by doubling its internal char buffer array, but) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 342 Td (  still allocates multiple arrays during growth. `string.Create\(\)` allocates the exact) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 331 Td (  target string memory once on the heap and passes a writable `Span<char>` to populate it) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 320 Td (  without any intermediate buffer or allocation\).*) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 45 307 Td (---) Tj ET
BT /F2 10 Tf 0.2 0.2 0.3 rg 45 291 Td (Q004: Structs vs Classes vs Records \(`class` vs `struct`\)) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 276 Td (* **Expected Depth**: Senior) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 263 Td (* **Key Answer Points**:) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 250 Td (* **`class`**: Reference type, identity-based equality by default, supports inheritance,) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 239 Td (  allocated on Heap, collected by GC.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 226 Td (* **`struct`**: Value type, allocated inline, value-based equality implemented via) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 215 Td (  reflection in `System.ValueType.Equals` \(unless overridden, causing performance-degrading) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 204 Td (  boxing\), no inheritance \(can implement interfaces\).) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 191 Td (* **`record class` \(or `record`\)**: Reference type with compiler-synthesized value-based) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 180 Td (  equality \(`IEquatable<T>`\), hash code calculation, `ToString\(\)` formatting,) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 169 Td (  non-destructive mutation via `with` expressions, and auto-generated deconstructors.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 156 Td (* **`record struct`**: Value type with compiler-synthesized value-based equality that) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 145 Td (  avoids `System.ValueType` reflection overhead. Can be declared `readonly record struct`) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 134 Td (  for guaranteed immutability.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 121 Td (* **The Trap**: Candidates confusing record equality with class equality, or assuming) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 110 Td (  `record` is a value type. By default, `record` is a `record class` \(reference type\).) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 97 Td (* **Follow-up Question**: Why is the default `ValueType.Equals\(\)` notorious for poor) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 86 Td (  performance, and how does the CLR internally determine if it can use `memcmp` \(fast) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 75 Td (  bitwise comparison\)? *\(Answer: If the struct contains any reference type fields or memory) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 64 Td (  padding bytes, `ValueType.Equals` falls back to reflection over all fields. If it has only) Tj ET
