BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 780 Td (  legacy libraries can freely pass `null`, crashing your code if you omit runtime guards) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 769 Td (  \(`ArgumentNullException.ThrowIfNull`\).) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 756 Td (* **Follow-up Question**: How does EF Core interpret `string` vs `string?` in entity) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 745 Td (  property definitions? *\(Answer: EF Core uses NRT annotations to configure database schema) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 734 Td (  nullability - `string` becomes `NOT NULL`, while `string?` generates a nullable column) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 723 Td (  `NULL` in SQL DDL migrations\).*) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 45 710 Td (---) Tj ET
BT /F2 10 Tf 0.2 0.2 0.3 rg 45 694 Td (Q008: `ref struct` and `readonly ref struct` Internals) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 679 Td (* **Expected Depth**: Senior) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 666 Td (* **Key Answer Points**:) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 653 Td (* A `ref struct` is a value type that is strictly stack-only. The CLR enforces at compile) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 642 Td (  time that an instance can never escape the current thread stack.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 629 Td (* **Enforced Constraints**:) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 616 Td (* Cannot be boxed to `object` or `System.ValueType`.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 603 Td (* Cannot implement interfaces \(prior to C# 13\).) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 590 Td (* Cannot be a field of a normal `class` or normal `struct`.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 577 Td (* Cannot be used in `async/await` methods across `await` suspension points \(because async) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 566 Td (  state machines are heap-allocated structs/classes\).) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 553 Td (* Cannot be captured in lambdas, delegates, or local functions.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 540 Td (* Cannot be used as generic type arguments \(prior to `allow ref struct` in C# 13\).) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 527 Td (* Used primarily for high-performance memory slicing types such as `Span<T>` and) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 516 Td (  `ReadOnlySpan<T>`.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 503 Td (* **The Trap**: Attempting to use `Span<T>` as a private field in an ASP.NET Core singleton) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 492 Td (  service or storing it inside an async method across an `await` call.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 479 Td (* **Follow-up Question**: Why does `Memory<T>` exist if `Span<T>` provides high-performance) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 468 Td (  memory slicing? *\(Answer: Because `Span<T>` is a `ref struct` constrained to the stack, it) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 457 Td (  cannot live on the heap. `Memory<T>` is a standard struct that can live on the heap,) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 446 Td (  allowing it to be stored in classes, used across async boundaries, and sliced into spans) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 435 Td (  on demand via `.Span`\).*) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 45 422 Td (---) Tj ET
BT /F2 10 Tf 0.2 0.2 0.3 rg 45 406 Td (Q009: Struct Mutation Traps and Defensive Copying) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 391 Td (* **Expected Depth**: Senior) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 378 Td (* **Key Answer Points**:) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 365 Td (* Structs are passed by value \(a full bitwise copy is pushed to the stack\).) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 352 Td (* When a struct is accessed through a `readonly` reference \(such as `in` parameter or) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 341 Td (  `readonly` field\), the C# compiler guarantees that the original struct cannot be modified.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 328 Td (* **Defensive Copying**: If you invoke a method or property getter on a non-readonly struct) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 317 Td (  stored in a `readonly` field or passed via `in`, the compiler has no guarantee that the) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 306 Td (  method won't mutate internal state. Therefore, it silently creates a defensive copy of the) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 295 Td (  struct on the stack and executes the method on the copy, discarding changes and consuming) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 284 Td (  CPU cycles.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 271 Td (* **Prevention**: Mark the struct itself as `readonly struct`. This informs the compiler) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 260 Td (  that no member can mutate state, eliminating the need for defensive copies.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 247 Td (* **The Trap**: Using mutable structs with `in` parameters to "save memory" in) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 236 Td (  high-frequency trading or math routines. If the struct is not `readonly`, method calls) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 225 Td (  create millions of hidden stack copies per second, worsening performance.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 212 Td (* **Follow-up Question**: What happens when you mutate a struct inside an array using an) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 201 Td (  indexer vs a foreach loop? *\(Answer: Mutating `array[i].Field = 5` directly updates the) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 190 Td (  memory in-place inside the array's contiguous memory block. Mutating via a `foreach` loop) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 179 Td (  is a compiler error because iteration variables are read-only copies\).*) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 45 166 Td (---) Tj ET
BT /F2 10 Tf 0.2 0.2 0.3 rg 45 150 Td (Q010: Garbage Collector Fundamentals: Roots, Generations, and Collection Triggers) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 135 Td (* **Expected Depth**: Intermediate) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 122 Td (* **Key Answer Points**:) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 109 Td (* The .NET GC is a generational, tracing garbage collector. It works on the premise of the) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 98 Td (  **Weak Generational Hypothesis**: recently created objects have the shortest lifetimes,) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 87 Td (  and older objects live longer.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 74 Td (* **Generations**:) Tj ET
