BT /F2 10 Tf 0.2 0.2 0.3 rg 45 740 Td (Q013: Method Overriding vs Method Hiding \(`override` vs `new`\)) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 725 Td (* **Expected Depth**: Intermediate) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 712 Td (* **Key Answer Points**:) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 699 Td (* **`virtual` / `override` \(Polymorphism\)**: Dynamic dispatch via the class's Virtual) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 688 Td (  Method Table \(`vtable`\). The runtime inspects the actual object type on the heap to) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 677 Td (  execute the most derived implementation, regardless of the reference type of the variable.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 664 Td (* **`new` \(Hiding / Shadowing\)**: Static dispatch determined by the compile-time type of) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 653 Td (  the reference pointer. It breaks the polymorphism chain: calling the method through a base) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 642 Td (  class reference executes the base implementation; calling through the derived reference) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 631 Td (  executes the derived implementation.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 618 Td (* **The Trap**: Believing `new` overrides the behavior of the base class. If a framework) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 607 Td (  invokes the method through a base class pointer or interface, the derived `new`) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 596 Td (  implementation is completely bypassed.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 583 Td (* **Follow-up Question**: What happens if an interface method is implemented implicitly in) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 572 Td (  a base class, and a derived class defines a method with the same signature using `new`?) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 561 Td (  *\(Answer: An interface reference pointing to the derived instance will still invoke the) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 550 Td (  base class implementation unless the derived class explicitly re-implements the) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 539 Td (  interface\).*) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 45 526 Td (---) Tj ET
BT /F2 10 Tf 0.2 0.2 0.3 rg 45 510 Td (Q014: Exception Handling Internals: Cost of Exceptions and `throw` vs `throw ex`) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 495 Td (* **Expected Depth**: Basic) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 482 Td (* **Key Answer Points**:) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 469 Td (* **`throw`**: Re-throws the original exception preserving the full call stack and source) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 458 Td (  code line numbers from the original point of failure.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 445 Td (* **`throw ex`**: Resets the stack trace to the current method line, permanently destroying) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 434 Td (  diagnostic information regarding where the exception originated in nested layers.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 421 Td (* **Exception Cost**: Throwing exceptions is computationally expensive because the CLR must) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 410 Td (  traverse the managed call stack, construct the stack frame string, locate catch handlers) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 399 Td (  across two passes \(Pass 1: search for handler; Pass 2: unwind stack and execute finally) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 388 Td (  blocks\). Never use exceptions for normal control flow \(use `TryParse` or `Result<T>`) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 377 Td (  pattern\).) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 364 Td (* **The Trap**: Using `throw ex` inside a catch block to "log and rethrow", destroying) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 353 Td (  production troubleshooting capability.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 340 Td (* **Follow-up Question**: What are exception filters \(`catch \(Exception ex\) when \(...\)`\),) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 329 Td (  and why are they performant compared to catching and re-throwing? *\(Answer: Exception) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 318 Td (  filters execute during the first pass of exception handling before the stack is unwound.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 307 Td (  If the condition is false, the stack remains intact without entering the catch block or) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 296 Td (  unwinding frames\).*) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 45 283 Td (---) Tj ET
BT /F2 10 Tf 0.2 0.2 0.3 rg 45 267 Td (Q015: Enums, Bitwise Flags, and Underlying Storage Types) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 252 Td (* **Expected Depth**: Intermediate) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 239 Td (* **Key Answer Points**:) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 226 Td (* An `enum` is a distinct value type whose underlying representation defaults to) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 215 Td (  `System.Int32` \(`int`\), but can explicitly inherit from `byte`, `sbyte`, `short`,) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 204 Td (  `ushort`, `int`, `uint`, `long`, or `ulong`.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 191 Td (* Decorated with `[Flags]` attribute to enable bitwise combination of values using binary) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 180 Td (  OR \(`|`\), AND \(`&`\), and XOR \(`^`\).) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 167 Td (* Flag values must be powers of two \($2^0, 2^1, 2^2, 2^3 \\dots$ i.e., $1, 2, 4, 8, 16$\).) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 154 Td (* Checking flags: Use `enum.HasFlag\(flag\)` \(optimized in modern .NET JIT to avoid boxing\)) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 143 Td (  or raw bitwise operators `\(val & flag\) == flag`.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 130 Td (* **The Trap**: Defining flag values linearly \($1, 2, 3, 4, 5$\). Setting value $3$) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 119 Td (  unintentionally activates both bits $1$ and $2$, causing logical bugs in permission) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 108 Td (  checks.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 95 Td (* **Follow-up Question**: Can an enum variable hold a numeric value that is not explicitly) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 84 Td (  defined in its declaration? *\(Answer: Yes, enums are essentially strongly typed numbers;) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 73 Td (  any integer within the underlying type range can be cast to the enum without throwing an) Tj ET
