BT /F3 7.5 Tf 0.1 0.15 0.3 rg 52 424 Td (    ref struct Span<T> {) Tj ET
BT /F3 7.5 Tf 0.1 0.15 0.3 rg 52 414.5 Td (        internal readonly ref T _pointer;) Tj ET
BT /F3 7.5 Tf 0.1 0.15 0.3 rg 52 405.0 Td (        private readonly int _length;) Tj ET
BT /F3 7.5 Tf 0.1 0.15 0.3 rg 52 395.5 Td (    }) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 379.0 Td (* Allows zero-allocation slicing \(`span.Slice\(start, length\)`\): no new memory is allocated;) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 368.0 Td (  it simply calculates a new memory offset and length.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 355.0 Td (* **Bounds Checking**: Enforced by the JIT compiler, with zero-cost bounds checking) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 344.0 Td (  elimination in predictable loops.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 331.0 Td (* **The Trap**: Confusing `Span<T>` with an array. `Span<T>` does not own the memory; it is) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 320.0 Td (  a temporary viewing window. If the underlying unmanaged buffer is freed or the stack frame) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 309.0 Td (  pops, the span points to corrupted memory.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 296.0 Td (* **Follow-up Question**: How does `MemoryExtensions.AsSpan\(\)` optimize string operations) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 285.0 Td (  like parsing an ISO 8601 date string? *\(Answer: Slices substrings out of the original) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 274.0 Td (  string as `ReadOnlySpan<char>` without allocating a single intermediate string object on) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 263.0 Td (  the heap, allowing zero-allocation parsing with `int.Parse` or `DateTime.Parse`\).*) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 45 250.0 Td (---) Tj ET
BT /F2 10 Tf 0.2 0.2 0.3 rg 45 234.0 Td (Q023: Method Parameter Modifiers: `ref`, `out`, `in`) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 219.0 Td (* **Expected Depth**: Intermediate) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 206.0 Td (* **Key Answer Points**:) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 193.0 Td (* **`ref`**: Passes an argument by reference. Must be initialized by the caller before) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 182.0 Td (  being passed into the method. Can be read and modified by the callee.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 169.0 Td (* **`out`**: Passes an argument by reference for output. Does *not* need to be initialized) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 158.0 Td (  by the caller. Must be assigned a value by the callee before the method returns.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 145.0 Td (* **`in`**: Passes an argument by reference read-only. Must be initialized by the caller.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 134.0 Td (  Cannot be modified by the callee. Designed to eliminate copying overhead for large) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 123.0 Td (  `readonly struct` parameters.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 110.0 Td (* **The Trap**: Using `in` parameters with small primitive types like `int` or `bool`.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 99.0 Td (  Passing an 8-byte reference pointer for a 4-byte `int` is slower than passing the value) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 88.0 Td (  directly on CPU registers.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 75.0 Td (* **Follow-up Question**: What is `ref readonly` return, and where is it used in the BCL?) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 64.0 Td (  *\(Answer: Returns a reference to internal memory without allowing the caller to mutate it;) Tj ET
