BT /F1 9 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 45 767 Td (---) Tj ET
BT /F2 10 Tf 0.2 0.2 0.3 rg 45 751 Td (Q024: Garbage Collection: Large Object Heap \(LOH\) Fragmentation and Pinned Object Heap \(POH\)) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 736 Td (* **Expected Depth**: Senior) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 723 Td (* **Key Answer Points**:) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 710 Td (* **LOH**: Holds objects $\\ge$ 85,000 bytes \(mostly large byte buffers, large strings, and) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 699 Td (  arrays\).) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 686 Td (* **Fragmentation**: LOH is historically collected during Gen 2 without compaction because) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 675 Td (  moving multi-megabyte memory blocks is CPU-prohibitive. This leaves free memory "holes".) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 664 Td (  If a new large object cannot fit into existing holes, the heap expands, leading to) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 653 Td (  `OutOfMemoryException` even when total free memory appears sufficient.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 640 Td (* Compaction can be triggered programmatically via) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 629 Td (  `GCSettings.LargeObjectHeapCompactionMode = GCLargeObjectHeapCompactionMode.CompactOnce`.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 616 Td (* **POH \(Pinned Object Heap\)**: Introduced in .NET 5. Pinned objects prevent the GC from) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 605 Td (  moving them during compaction \(used in socket buffers and native OS interop\). Allocating) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 594 Td (  pinned objects on SOH prevents compaction of neighboring objects; POH isolates them) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 583 Td (  entirely.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 570 Td (* **The Trap**: Allocating large byte arrays repeatedly in high-throughput network or file) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 559 Td (  processing code. Use `ArrayPool<T>.Shared.Rent\(\)` to reuse memory buffers and completely) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 548 Td (  avoid LOH churn.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 535 Td (* **Follow-up Question**: How does `ArrayPool<T>.Shared` handle buffer sizes when you) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 524 Td (  request an array of length $1000$? *\(Answer: It rounds up to the next power of two or) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 513 Td (  bucket size, returning an array larger than requested. Developers must slice it to the) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 502 Td (  requested length and return it via `.Return\(\)` when finished\).*) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 45 489 Td (---) Tj ET
BT /F2 10 Tf 0.2 0.2 0.3 rg 45 473 Td (Q025: The `async` / `await` State Machine Under the Hood) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 458 Td (* **Expected Depth**: Senior) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 445 Td (* **Key Answer Points**:) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 432 Td (* The C# compiler transforms an `async` method into an implementation of) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 421 Td (  `IAsyncStateMachine`.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 408 Td (* The method body is broken into a switch statement inside the `MoveNext\(\)` method, where) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 397 Td (  each `await` expression represents a state transition.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 384 Td (* **Execution Sequence**:) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 371 Td (* Method begins running synchronously on the calling thread until it encounters the first) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 360 Td (  `await` on an incomplete awaitable \(`awaiter.IsCompleted == false`\).) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 347 Td (* The state machine captures the execution context / continuation.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 334 Td (* `awaiter.UnsafeOnCompleted\(continuation\)` registers a callback to invoke `MoveNext\(\)`) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 323 Td (  when the asynchronous operation completes.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 310 Td (* The calling thread returns immediately with an incomplete `Task`.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 297 Td (* When the background I/O or operation finishes, the OS/threadpool notifies the runtime,) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 286 Td (  which schedules `MoveNext\(\)` on an available thread to resume execution.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 273 Td (* **The Trap**: Thinking `async` creates a new thread. Pure asynchronous operations \(like) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 262 Td (  network sockets or file reads via OS I/O completion ports / IOCP\) consume zero threads) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 251 Td (  while awaiting.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 238 Td (* **Follow-up Question**: What is the memory cost of the compiler-generated async state) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 227 Td (  machine, and how did Microsoft optimize it in modern .NET? *\(Answer: For `async Task`, the) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 216 Td (  state machine is allocated on the heap if it yields; in .NET Core/6+,) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 205 Td (  `AsyncMethodBuilderCache` and pooled builder types reduce or eliminate these allocations) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 194 Td (  for hot paths\).*) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 45 181 Td (---) Tj ET
BT /F2 10 Tf 0.2 0.2 0.3 rg 45 165 Td (Q026: `SynchronizationContext` and `ConfigureAwait\(false\)`) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 150 Td (* **Expected Depth**: Senior) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 137 Td (* **Key Answer Points**:) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 124 Td (* `SynchronizationContext` provides a mechanism to marshal a delegate to a specific) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 113 Td (  threading environment \(e.g., UI thread in WPF/WinForms\).) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 100 Td (* **ASP.NET Core Reality**: ASP.NET Core has **no** `SynchronizationContext`. All) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 89 Td (  continuations run on the standard ThreadPool.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 76 Td (* **`ConfigureAwait\(false\)`**: Instructs the awaiter not to capture the current) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 65 Td (  `SynchronizationContext` or `TaskScheduler`, resuming the continuation on any available) Tj ET
BT /F1 8 Tf 0.4 0.4 0.4 rg
45 25 Td (INFOSYS & ENTERPRISE .NET INTERVIEW PREPARATION GUIDE  |  Page 70) Tj ET
