BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 780 Td (  ThreadPool thread.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 767 Td (* **Best Practice**: In library code \(NuGet packages\), always use `ConfigureAwait\(false\)`) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 756 Td (  to prevent deadlocks in legacy UI or classic ASP.NET applications that have a) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 745 Td (  single-threaded `SynchronizationContext`. In ASP.NET Core application controller/service) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 734 Td (  code, `ConfigureAwait\(false\)` is redundant because no `SynchronizationContext` exists.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 721 Td (* **The Trap**: Believing that `ConfigureAwait\(false\)` guarantees execution on a) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 710 Td (  *different* thread. If the awaited task is already completed when evaluated \(`IsCompleted) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 699 Td (  == true`\), execution continues synchronously on the *same* thread.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 686 Td (* **Follow-up Question**: Why does `ConfigureAwait\(false\)` not eliminate deadlocks if) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 675 Td (  calling code blocks using `.Result` or `.Wait\(\)` on classic .NET Framework ASP.NET?) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 664 Td (  *\(Answer: If the method begins execution on the synchronization context and waits) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 653 Td (  synchronously on an inner async method that wasn't decorated with `ConfigureAwait\(false\)`,) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 642 Td (  the inner method attempts to resume on the blocked context, causing a deadlock\).*) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 45 629 Td (---) Tj ET
BT /F2 10 Tf 0.2 0.2 0.3 rg 45 613 Td (Q027: `ValueTask<T>` vs `Task<T>`: Performance vs Hazards) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 598 Td (* **Expected Depth**: Senior) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 585 Td (* **Key Answer Points**:) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 572 Td (* `Task<T>` is a reference type. Instantiating it requires a heap allocation every time a) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 561 Td (  method executes asynchronously.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 548 Td (* `ValueTask<T>` is a discriminated union `struct` that holds either a completed result) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 537 Td (  \(`TResult`\) or a `Task<T>` / `IValueTaskSource<T>`.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 524 Td (* **When to Use**: Ideal for high-throughput methods that complete synchronously $90\\%+$ of) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 513 Td (  the time \(e.g., reading from an in-memory cache, socket buffer with existing data\). If) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 502 Td (  completed synchronously, zero heap allocation occurs.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 489 Td (* **The Hazardous Traps of `ValueTask`**:) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 476 Td (* **Never await a `ValueTask` multiple times**: The underlying `IValueTaskSource` may be) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 465 Td (  returned to an object pool and reused. Multiple awaits cause data corruption or crashes.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 452 Td (* **Never call `.AsTask\(\)` after awaiting**: Consume it once.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 439 Td (* **Never await concurrently** using `Task.WhenAll` without converting via `.AsTask\(\)`.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 426 Td (* **The Trap**: Defaulting to `ValueTask` for all async methods. If the operation) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 415 Td (  frequently completes asynchronously, wrapping it in `ValueTask` is slower and larger) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 404 Td (  \(struct overhead\) than returning a standard `Task`.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 391 Td (* **Follow-up Question**: How does `IValueTaskSource` enable zero-allocation asynchronous) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 380 Td (  operations that complete asynchronously? *\(Answer: It allows custom reusable, pooled) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 369 Td (  objects to back the `ValueTask`, resetting their state upon completion rather than) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 358 Td (  allocating a new `Task` instance\).*) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 45 345 Td (---) Tj ET
BT /F2 10 Tf 0.2 0.2 0.3 rg 45 329 Td (Q028: ThreadPool Starvation and Sync-Over-Async Deadlocks) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 314 Td (* **Expected Depth**: Senior) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 301 Td (* **Key Answer Points**:) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 288 Td (* The .NET ThreadPool manages worker threads to execute asynchronous callbacks, timers, and) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 277 Td (  queued tasks.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 264 Td (* **Sync-over-Async Antipattern**: Blocking on asynchronous tasks using `.Result`,) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 253 Td (  `.Wait\(\)`, or `.GetAwaiter\(\).GetResult\(\)` in thread pool threads.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 240 Td (* **Starvation Mechanism**:) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 227 Td (* Thread A from ThreadPool executes an action that calls `.Result` on an async task. Thread) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 216 Td (  A is now blocked, waiting for the task to complete.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 203 Td (* The asynchronous task finishes its I/O and needs a ThreadPool worker to execute its) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 192 Td (  continuation.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 179 Td (* If all ThreadPool threads are similarly blocked waiting on `.Result`, no thread is) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 168 Td (  available to run the continuation.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 155 Td (* The ThreadPool has a hill-climbing algorithm that injects new threads slowly \(e.g., 1-2) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 144 Td (  threads per 500ms\).) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 131 Td (* Incoming HTTP requests queue up; response times spike to 30+ seconds, culminating in) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 120 Td (  total service outage.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 107 Td (* **The Trap**: Thinking that wrapping blocking code in `Task.Run\(\(\) =>) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 96 Td (  LongRunningBlockingCall\(\)\)` solves ThreadPool starvation. It actually burns another) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 85 Td (  ThreadPool thread! Use proper async APIs or configure dedicated threads.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 72 Td (* **Follow-up Question**: How do you identify ThreadPool starvation in a production memory) Tj ET
BT /F1 8 Tf 0.4 0.4 0.4 rg
