BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 780 Td (  dump using `dotnet-dump`? *\(Answer: Run `clrthreads` and observe a massive queue of work) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 769 Td (  items with thread pool worker threads sitting blocked on `Monitor.Wait` or) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 758 Td (  `ManualResetEventSlim`\).*) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 45 745 Td (---) Tj ET
BT /F2 10 Tf 0.2 0.2 0.3 rg 45 729 Td (Q029: Asynchronous Concurrency: `Task.WhenAll` vs `Parallel.ForEachAsync`) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 714 Td (* **Expected Depth**: Intermediate) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 701 Td (* **Key Answer Points**:) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 688 Td (* **`Task.WhenAll`**: Starts all tasks simultaneously in an unbounded fashion and waits for) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 677 Td (  all to complete. If you pass an array of 5,000 tasks to `Task.WhenAll`, all 5,000 execute) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 666 Td (  concurrently, easily exhausting outbound socket connections, database connection pools, or) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 655 Td (  downstream API rate limits.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 642 Td (* **`Parallel.ForEachAsync`** \(Introduced in .NET 6\): Provides bounded, throttled) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 631 Td (  asynchronous parallelism. Accepts a `MaxDegreeOfParallelism` setting to limit concurrent) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 620 Td (  executions \(e.g., maximum 10 tasks at a time\) and handles graceful cancellation via) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 609 Td (  `CancellationToken`.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 596 Td (* **The Trap**: Exception handling with `Task.WhenAll`. If multiple tasks throw exceptions,) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 585 Td (  `Task.WhenAll` encapsulates them in an `AggregateException`. However, when using `await) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 574 Td (  Task.WhenAll\(tasks\)`, the `await` keyword unpacks and re-throws only the *first*) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 563 Td (  exception. The other exceptions are lost unless inspected on the task directly.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 550 Td (* **Follow-up Question**: How do you throttle concurrent asynchronous calls in .NET if you) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 539 Td (  are working with an `IAsyncEnumerable<T>` stream? *\(Answer: Use `SemaphoreSlim` with an) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 528 Td (  initial count set to the desired concurrency limit, wrapping each unit of work\).*) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 45 515 Td (---) Tj ET
BT /F2 10 Tf 0.2 0.2 0.3 rg 45 499 Td (Q030: Volatile Read/Write, `Thread.MemoryBarrier`, and CPU Instruction Reordering) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 484 Td (* **Expected Depth**: Senior) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 471 Td (* **Key Answer Points**:) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 458 Td (* Modern CPUs and JIT compilers aggressively reorder assembly instructions to maximize) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 447 Td (  instruction pipeline throughput and cache hits, provided single-threaded logic semantics) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 436 Td (  are preserved.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 423 Td (* In multi-threaded environments, instruction reordering can cause thread B to observe) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 412 Td (  memory updates from thread A out of order.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 399 Td (* **`volatile` keyword**: Tells the compiler and CPU that a field can be modified by) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 388 Td (  multiple concurrent threads. Enforces an acquire-fence on read \(prevents later) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 377 Td (  reads/writes from moving before it\) and a release-fence on write \(prevents earlier) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 366 Td (  reads/writes from moving after it\).) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 353 Td (* **`Thread.MemoryBarrier\(\)`**: Inserts a full memory fence: no processor reads or writes) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 342 Td (  can cross the barrier in either direction.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 329 Td (* Modern alternatives: `Interlocked` methods and `Volatile.Read` / `Volatile.Write`.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 316 Td (* **The Trap**: Using `volatile` to synchronize compound operations like `counter++`.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 305 Td (  `volatile` only guarantees memory visibility; it does *not* provide atomicity \(increment) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 294 Td (  requires read, modify, write\).) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 281 Td (* **Follow-up Question**: Why does double-checked locking require the singleton instance) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 270 Td (  variable to be `volatile` in C#? *\(Answer: To prevent the CPU from publishing the) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 259 Td (  reference pointer to other threads before the constructor has finished initializing the) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 248 Td (  object's fields\).*) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 45 235 Td (---) Tj ET
BT /F2 10 Tf 0.2 0.2 0.3 rg 45 219 Td (Q031: `Interlocked` Atomic Operations vs `lock` \(Monitor\)) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 204 Td (* **Expected Depth**: Intermediate) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 191 Td (* **Key Answer Points**:) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 178 Td (* **`lock\(obj\)`**: Implemented using `Monitor.Enter` and `Monitor.Exit`. A pessimistic,) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 167 Td (  heavyweight synchronization primitive that halts contending threads in the OS kernel wait) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 156 Td (  queue, incurring thread context switching overhead.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 143 Td (* **`Interlocked`**: Provides atomic hardware-level primitives implemented directly via CPU) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 132 Td (  bus lock instructions \(`LOCK CMPXCHG` on x86/x64\). Zero kernel transitions, non-blocking,) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 121 Td (  lock-free.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 108 Td (* Methods: `Interlocked.Increment`, `Interlocked.Decrement`, `Interlocked.Add`,) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 97 Td (  `Interlocked.Exchange`, and `Interlocked.CompareExchange` \(atomic compare-and-swap\).) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 84 Td (* **The Trap**: Locking on `this`, `typeof\(Class\)`, or a `string` literal. These objects) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 73 Td (  can be accessed outside your control, leading to accidental deadlocks across components.) Tj ET
