BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 496 Td (* **Expected Depth**: Intermediate) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 483 Td (* **Key Answer Points**:) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 470 Td (* SQL Server stores indexes as multi-level B-Tree structures \(Root, Intermediate, Leaf) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 459 Td (  levels\) in 8KB data pages.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 446 Td (* **Clustered Index**:) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 433 Td (* Determines the physical storage order of the data rows in the table.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 420 Td (* The **Leaf Level IS the data**. A table can have only **one** clustered index.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 407 Td (* If a table has no clustered index, it is stored as an unordered **Heap**.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 394 Td (* **Non-Clustered Index**:) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 381 Td (* A separate B-Tree structure containing only the indexed key columns and a row locator.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 368 Td (* The **Row Locator**: If the table has a clustered index, the locator is the Clustered) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 357 Td (  Index Key. If the table is a Heap, the locator is a Row Identifier \(`RID`: FileID + PageID) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 346 Td (  + Slot\).) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 333 Td (* **Key Lookup / RID Lookup**: When a non-clustered index does not contain all columns) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 322 Td (  requested by a query, the engine must perform a lookup into the clustered index/heap for) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 311 Td (  every matching row, causing expensive random I/O.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 298 Td (* **The Trap**: Using a `GUID` \(`uniqueidentifier`\) generated via `Guid.NewGuid\(\)` as a) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 287 Td (  clustered primary key. Non-sequential GUIDs cause continuous **Page Splits**, high index) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 276 Td (  fragmentation, and severe disk I/O churn. Use sequential GUIDs \(`NEWSEQUENTIALID\(\)`\) or) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 265 Td (  integer `IDENTITY` / `BIGINT`.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 252 Td (* **Follow-up Question**: What is the depth of a typical B-Tree in a table with 10 million) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 241 Td (  rows, and how many page reads does a seek require? *\(Answer: Typically 3 to 4 levels deep;) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 230 Td (  an index seek requires only 3 to 4 logical page reads to reach the leaf data\).*) Tj ET
