BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 529 Td (* **Expected Depth**: Senior) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 516 Td (* **Key Answer Points**:) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 503 Td (* **Locks**: High-level logical synchronization primitives protecting logical data \(rows,) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 492 Td (  keys, pages, tables\) to enforce transaction isolation. Managed by the Lock Manager. Can be) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 481 Td (  held for long transaction durations.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 468 Td (* **Latches**: Internal, lightweight synchronization primitives protecting physical) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 457 Td (  in-memory data structures \(8KB buffer pool pages\) from concurrent thread modification.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 446 Td (  Held for micro-durations; developers have no direct control over latches.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 433 Td (* **Spinlocks**: Ultra-lightweight busy-wait CPU loops protecting internal memory pointers) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 422 Td (  when lock wait times are expected to be nanoseconds.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 409 Td (* **Deadlock Mechanics**:) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 396 Td (* Occurs when two or more sessions hold resources the other needs in a circular dependency) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 385 Td (  chain \(e.g., Session 1 holds Lock A, wants Lock B; Session 2 holds Lock B, wants Lock A\).) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 372 Td (* SQL Server Lock Monitor thread runs periodically \(every 5 seconds, scaling down to 100ms) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 361 Td (  when detected\), identifies cycles, selects a **Victim** \(transaction with lowest rollback) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 350 Td (  cost or priority\), rolls it back, and throws error 1205.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 337 Td (* **The Trap**: Handling deadlocks by simply increasing command timeouts. Timeout does not) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 326 Td (  resolve deadlocks; you must implement exponential backoff retry policies in C# and enforce) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 315 Td (  consistent object access ordering in SQL.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 302 Td (* **Follow-up Question**: What key information do you look for in an XML Deadlock Graph) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 291 Td (  \(`system_health` session or Extended Events\)? *\(Answer: Look at the `<victim-list>`,) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 280 Td (  `<process-list>` \(identifying input buffer queries, isolation levels, and SPIDs\), and) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 269 Td (  `<resource-list>` \(identifying competing locks on specific index keys or pages\)\).*) Tj ET
