BT /F2 10 Tf 0.2 0.2 0.3 rg 45 780 Td (Q101: gRPC vs REST with JSON in High-Throughput Microservices) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 765 Td (* **Expected Depth**: Intermediate) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 752 Td (* **Key Answer Points**:) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 739 Td (* **REST with JSON**:) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 726 Td (* Relies on HTTP/1.1 \(predominantly\), human-readable text-based JSON, stateless) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 715 Td (  request/response.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 702 Td (* High payload overhead \(string field names repeated on every message\), expensive CPU) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 691 Td (  serialization/deserialization.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 678 Td (* Ideal for: Public-facing APIs, web browser frontends, third-party integrations.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 665 Td (* **gRPC**:) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 652 Td (* Built on **HTTP/2**: Multiplexes hundreds of concurrent requests over a single TCP) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 641 Td (  connection, eliminating head-of-line blocking, TCP handshakes, and connection pool) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 630 Td (  exhaustion. Supports HTTP/3 / QUIC in modern .NET.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 617 Td (* Binary Serialization via **Protocol Buffers \(Protobuf\)**: Strongly typed, schema-first,) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 606 Td (  compact binary format \(up to 70-80% smaller payloads, 5-10x faster CPU serialization\).) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 593 Td (* Supports full **Bi-directional Streaming** \(Client, Server, Duplex\).) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 580 Td (* Ideal for: East-West \(internal service-to-service\) communication where low latency and) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 569 Td (  high throughput are paramount.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 556 Td (* **The Trap**: Attempting to use gRPC directly in standard web browsers without gRPC-Web) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 545 Td (  proxy support. Standard browsers do not expose low-level HTTP/2 framing controls required) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 534 Td (  by gRPC.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 521 Td (* **Follow-up Question**: How does Protobuf achieve backward and forward schema) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 510 Td (  compatibility when fields are added or removed? *\(Answer: By relying on unique numerical) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 499 Td (  Field Tags rather than field names; unknown tags are preserved or skipped during) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 488 Td (  deserialization\).*) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 45 475 Td (---) Tj ET
BT /F2 10 Tf 0.2 0.2 0.3 rg 45 459 Td (Q102: Message Brokers: RabbitMQ vs Apache Kafka Architecture) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 444 Td (* **Expected Depth**: Senior) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 431 Td (* **Key Answer Points**:) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 45 418 Td (Dimension | RabbitMQ | Apache Kafka) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 45 402 Td (**Paradigm** | Smart Broker, Dumb Consumer \(Message Queue\) | Dumb Broker, Smart Consumer) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 45 390 Td (\(Distributed Commit Log\)) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 45 374 Td (**Routing** | Highly flexible \(Direct, Topic, Fanout, Headers via Exchanges\) | Partition key) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 45 362 Td (hashing directly to log partitions) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 45 346 Td (**Message Consumption** | Destructive: broker removes message once ACKed by consumer |) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 45 334 Td (Non-destructive: messages persist on disk according to retention policy) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 45 318 Td (**Ordering** | FIFO within a single queue; broken if parallel consumers act | Strict ordering) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 45 306 Td (guaranteed **per partition**) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 45 290 Td (**Consumer Scaling** | Competing Consumers pattern on a single queue | Consumer Groups:) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 45 278 Td (maximum active consumers = number of partitions) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 45 262 Td (**Replayability** | Cannot replay messages once acknowledged | Consumers can reset offsets and) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 45 250 Td (replay historic data streams) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 234 Td (* **When to Use RabbitMQ**: Transactional commands, request-reply patterns, complex) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 223 Td (  routing, task queues with immediate deletion.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 210 Td (* **When to Use Kafka**: High-throughput event streaming \(millions/sec\), telemetry,) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 199 Td (  real-time analytics, event sourcing, CDC \(Change Data Capture\).) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 186 Td (* **The Trap**: Adding more consumers to a Kafka consumer group than there are partitions.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 175 Td (  The excess consumers sit completely idle doing zero work.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 162 Td (* **Follow-up Question**: What happens in RabbitMQ if a consumer throws an unhandled) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 151 Td (  exception without acknowledging or rejecting a message? *\(Answer: The TCP socket closes,) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 140 Td (  RabbitMQ marks the message as unacknowledged and requeues it at the head of the queue,) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 129 Td (  potentially causing infinite poison message loops\).*) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 45 116 Td (---) Tj ET
