BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 780 Td (* **Command Query Responsibility Segregation \(CQRS\)** splits an application's data) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 769 Td (  operations into two models:) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 756 Td (* **Commands**: Mutate state \(`CreateOrderCommand`, `CancelOrderCommand`\). Return no data) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 745 Td (  or minimal status \(`Result<Guid>`\). Encapsulate business logic and validate domain) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 734 Td (  invariants.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 721 Td (* **Queries**: Read data \(`GetOrderByIdQuery`, `SearchOrdersQuery`\). Never mutate state.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 710 Td (  Return optimized DTOs directly.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 697 Td (* **MediatR Implementation**:) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 684 Td (* Commands implement `IRequest<Result>` and are handled by `IRequestHandler<TCommand,) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 673 Td (  Result>`.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 660 Td (* Queries implement `IRequest<OrderDto>` and are handled by `IRequestHandler<TQuery,) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 649 Td (  OrderDto>`.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 636 Td (* **Benefits**: Read and write paths can be optimized independently \(e.g., Writes use EF) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 625 Td (  Core with strict domain models; Reads use Dapper or EF Core with `AsNoTracking\(\)`) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 614 Td (  executing raw SQL views\).) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 601 Td (* **The Trap**: Implementing CQRS by forcing separate physical read/write databases with) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 590 Td (  asynchronous sync for simple CRUD applications. CQRS can be implemented logically within a) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 579 Td (  single database using separate read/write models.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 566 Td (* **Follow-up Question**: How do MediatR Pipeline Behaviors streamline cross-cutting) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 555 Td (  concerns in CQRS? *\(Answer: They wrap request handling with automated validation) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 544 Td (  [FluentValidation], distributed tracing, performance metrics, and transaction management) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 533 Td (  without cluttering handlers\).*) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 45 520 Td (---) Tj ET
BT /F2 10 Tf 0.2 0.2 0.3 rg 45 504 Td (Q104: Event Sourcing Mechanics: Aggregates, Projections, and Snapshots) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 489 Td (* **Expected Depth**: Senior) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 476 Td (* **Key Answer Points**:) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 463 Td (* Traditional systems persist the **current state** of an entity via `UPDATE` statements.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 450 Td (* **Event Sourcing**: Never updates or deletes data. Persists state as an immutable) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 439 Td (  sequence of domain events in an append-only **Event Store** \(`OrderPlaced`,) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 428 Td (  `OrderItemAdded`, `PaymentReceived`, `OrderShipped`\).) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 415 Td (* **Rehydration**: To load an aggregate, read all historic events for its `AggregateId`) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 404 Td (  from the store and replay them in chronological order through the aggregate's internal) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 393 Td (  `Apply\(Event\)` methods.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 380 Td (* **Projections \(Read Models\)**: Background consumers listen to committed events and) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 369 Td (  project them into read-optimized SQL or document databases \(denormalized views\) for fast) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 358 Td (  querying.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 345 Td (* **Snapshots**: If an aggregate has thousands of events \(e.g., bank account with 50,000) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 334 Td (  transactions\), rehydration becomes slow. Take a periodic snapshot \(every 100 events\)) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 323 Td (  storing current aggregate state; rehydration loads the snapshot and replays only) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 312 Td (  subsequent events.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 299 Td (* **The Trap**: Believing Event Sourcing requires CQRS and Microservices. While often) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 288 Td (  paired together, Event Sourcing is strictly a persistence pattern for domain aggregates.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 275 Td (* **Follow-up Question**: How do you handle schema changes / versioning to historical) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 264 Td (  domain events that occurred 3 years ago? *\(Answer: Using Upcasting: transforming older) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 253 Td (  event schema payloads into modern event contracts in memory during rehydration pipeline) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 242 Td (  before aggregate consumption\).*) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 45 229 Td (---) Tj ET
BT /F2 10 Tf 0.2 0.2 0.3 rg 45 213 Td (Q105: The Saga Pattern: Choreography vs Orchestration) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 198 Td (* **Expected Depth**: Senior) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 185 Td (* **Key Answer Points**:) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 172 Td (* Traditional ACID transactions cannot span multiple microservices \(2PC is slow, brittle,) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 161 Td (  and non-scalable\). Sagas manage distributed transactions as a sequence of local) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 150 Td (  transactions.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 137 Td (* If a local step fails, the Saga executes **Compensating Transactions** in reverse order) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 126 Td (  to undo changes \(e.g., `RefundPayment`, `ReleaseInventoryReservation`\).) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 113 Td (* **Choreography \(Event-Driven\)**:) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 100 Td (* Decentralized. Services publish domain events; other services listen and act.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 87 Td (* Pros: Simple for small workflows \(2-3 services\); loose coupling.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 74 Td (* Cons: Difficult to monitor; circular dependency risk; hard to understand end-to-end) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 63 Td (  state.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8 Tf 0.4 0.4 0.4 rg
45 25 Td (INFOSYS & ENTERPRISE .NET INTERVIEW PREPARATION GUIDE  |  Page 102) Tj ET
