BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 780 Td (* **Orchestration \(Centralized Coordinator\)**:) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 767 Td (* A dedicated Saga Orchestrator \(e.g., MassTransit Courier, Temporal, Camunda\) coordinates) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 756 Td (  the workflow. Sends explicit commands to services, tracks state in a State Machine) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 745 Td (  database, and initiates compensations upon failure.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 732 Td (* Pros: Centralized visibility, easy debugging, handles complex state transitions cleanly.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 719 Td (* Cons: Additional infrastructure; risk of centralizing too much business logic in the) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 708 Td (  orchestrator.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 695 Td (* **The Trap**: Assuming compensating transactions restore the system to an identical) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 684 Td (  previous state like a SQL `ROLLBACK`. Compensations are *new forward transactions* \(e.g.,) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 673 Td (  a refund charge appears on the customer's credit card statement; the original charge is) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 662 Td (  not erased\).) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 649 Td (* **Follow-up Question**: How does MassTransit State Machine \(Automatonymous\) persist Saga) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 638 Td (  state across asynchronous steps? *\(Answer: Using an entity mapped to a database [EF Core) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 627 Td (  or Redis] containing the `CorrelationId` and `CurrentState`, resuming state machine) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 616 Td (  execution upon receiving correlation events\).*) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 45 603 Td (---) Tj ET
BT /F2 10 Tf 0.2 0.2 0.3 rg 45 587 Td (Q106: The Outbox Pattern: Solving the Dual-Write Problem) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 572 Td (* **Expected Depth**: Senior) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 559 Td (* **Key Answer Points**:) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 546 Td (* **The Dual-Write Problem**: A business service must update its local database AND publish) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 535 Td (  an event to a message broker \(RabbitMQ/Kafka\).) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 522 Td (* If you write to the DB first, and the broker is unreachable or network drops $\\to$ DB is) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 511 Td (  updated, but event is never published \(Data inconsistency\).) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 498 Td (* If you publish to the broker first, and DB commit fails $\\to$ Event published, but DB has) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 487 Td (  no record \(Phantom event\).) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 474 Td (* **The Outbox Solution**:) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 461 Td (* Inside the **same local database transaction**, write the business entity changes to the) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 450 Td (  main table AND write the outgoing event message into an `Outbox` table.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 437 Td (* Because both writes share the same local ACID transaction, either both succeed or both) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 426 Td (  roll back atomically.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 413 Td (* An asynchronous background process reads unpublished records from the `Outbox` table and) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 402 Td (  publishes them to the message broker.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 389 Td (* Upon successful broker ACK, the outbox record is marked published or deleted.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 376 Td (* **Publishing Mechanisms**: Polling Publisher \(periodic background query\) or Transaction) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 365 Td (  Log Tailing / Change Data Capture \(CDC via Debezium\).) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 352 Td (* **The Trap**: Assuming the Outbox pattern guarantees **Exactly-Once** delivery to) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 341 Td (  consumers. The Outbox pattern guarantees **At-Least-Once** delivery. Network failures) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 330 Td (  during broker ACK can cause duplicate publishes. Consumers must be **idempotent**.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 317 Td (* **Follow-up Question**: How does the open-source MassTransit transactional outbox) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 306 Td (  integrate seamlessly with EF Core? *\(Answer: It intercepts `SaveChangesAsync\(\)`, saves) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 295 Td (  outbox message entities into the same `DbContext` transaction, and uses a hosted service) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 284 Td (  to publish and sweep them\).*) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 45 271 Td (---) Tj ET
BT /F2 10 Tf 0.2 0.2 0.3 rg 45 255 Td (Q107: Polly Circuit Breaker: States, Transitions, and Configuration) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 240 Td (* **Expected Depth**: Senior) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 227 Td (* **Key Answer Points**:) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 214 Td (* Prevents cascading failures when a downstream dependency \(API, Database, Third-Party) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 203 Td (  Gateway\) is failing or degraded.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 190 Td (* Three Primary States:) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 177 Td (* **Closed \(Normal Operation\)**: Requests pass through to downstream service. Failure) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 166 Td (  metrics \(timeouts, 500s\) are tracked over a rolling time window.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 153 Td (* **Open \(Tripped / Broken\)**: Failure threshold exceeded \(e.g., $50\\%$ failures over 30s\).) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 142 Td (  All requests are immediately short-circuited and fail-fast with `BrokenCircuitException`) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 131 Td (  without calling downstream service, allowing it time to recover.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 118 Td (* **Half-Open \(Probe / Trial\)**: After a sleep duration \(e.g., 30s\), a limited trial number) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 107 Td (  of requests are permitted through. If they succeed, circuit transitions back to) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 96 Td (  **Closed**; if any fails, circuit immediately reverts to **Open**.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 83 Td (* Advanced: **Advanced Circuit Breaker** requires a minimum throughput volume before) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 72 Td (  evaluating failure percentages to avoid tripping on a single isolated failure.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8 Tf 0.4 0.4 0.4 rg
45 25 Td (INFOSYS & ENTERPRISE .NET INTERVIEW PREPARATION GUIDE  |  Page 103) Tj ET
