BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 780 Td (* If miss, application queries database, writes data into cache with a TTL \(Time-To-Live\),) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 769 Td (  and returns data.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 756 Td (* Pros: Resilient against cache failure \(falls back to DB\); only caches requested data.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 743 Td (* Cons: Cache miss incurs two network hops; stale data risk if DB updates bypass cache) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 732 Td (  invalidation.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 719 Td (* **Write-Through**:) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 706 Td (* Application writes to cache; cache synchronously writes to database before returning) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 695 Td (  success.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 682 Td (* Pros: Cache is always up-to-date; zero stale data reads.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 669 Td (* Cons: Higher write latency \(two synchronous writes\).) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 656 Td (* **Write-Behind \(Write-Back\)**:) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 643 Td (* Application writes directly to cache; cache acknowledges immediately and queues) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 632 Td (  asynchronous batch writes to the database.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 619 Td (* Pros: Blazing fast write performance; absorbs massive traffic spikes.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 606 Td (* Cons: High risk of permanent data loss if cache crashes before queued writes are flushed) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 595 Td (  to database.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 582 Td (* **The Trap**: Using Cache-Aside without setting an absolute TTL expiration. If an update) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 571 Td (  mutation fails to invalidate the cache key, the stale data persists indefinitely.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 558 Td (* **Follow-up Question**: How do you avoid cache invalidation race conditions when an) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 547 Td (  entity updates in the database? *\(Answer: Delete the cache key after committing the) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 536 Td (  database transaction, rather than updating it, forcing a clean reload on next read\).*) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 45 523 Td (---) Tj ET
BT /F2 10 Tf 0.2 0.2 0.3 rg 45 507 Td (Q113: CAP Theorem vs PACELC Theorem in Distributed Architectures) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 492 Td (* **Expected Depth**: Senior) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 479 Td (* **Key Answer Points**:) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 466 Td (* **CAP Theorem**: In any asynchronous network subject to partitions \(**P**\), a distributed) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 455 Td (  system can guarantee at most two of:) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 442 Td (* **C \(Consistency / Linearizability\)**: Every read receives the most recent write or an) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 431 Td (  error.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 418 Td (* **A \(Availability\)**: Every non-failing node returns a non-error response, without) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 407 Td (  guarantee that it is the most recent write.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 394 Td (* **P \(Partition Tolerance\)**: The system continues to operate despite arbitrary network) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 383 Td (  message loss or network splits.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 370 Td (* Since network partitions are physical realities in distributed clouds, systems must) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 359 Td (  choose between **CP** \(e.g., MongoDB, Redis with Raft, Spanner\) or **AP** \(e.g.,) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 348 Td (  Cassandra, DynamoDB, Couchbase\).) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 335 Td (* **PACELC Theorem \(Extends CAP\)**:) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 322 Td (* If there is a **P**artition: trade off **A**vailability vs **C**onsistency.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 309 Td (* **E**lse \(normal operation\): trade off **L**atency vs **C**onsistency.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 296 Td (* E.g., MongoDB is PC/EC: favors consistency during partition, and favors consistency over) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 285 Td (  latency under normal operations. DynamoDB is PA/EL: favors availability and low latency.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 272 Td (* **The Trap**: Believing standard relational databases like SQL Server or PostgreSQL are) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 261 Td (  "CA". In a distributed cluster, if the network partitions between primary and secondary,) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 250 Td (  it must either reject writes \(CP\) or serve stale reads \(AP\). A network partition leaves no) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 239 Td (  room for CA.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 226 Td (* **Follow-up Question**: Why does an e-commerce shopping cart typically choose AP over CP?) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 215 Td (  *\(Answer: Business demands that a customer must always be able to add items to their cart) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 204 Td (  [high availability]; temporary minor inventory count discrepancies can be resolved later) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 193 Td (  before checkout\).*) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 45 180 Td (---) Tj ET
BT /F2 10 Tf 0.2 0.2 0.3 rg 45 164 Td (Q114: Two-Phase Commit \(2PC\) vs Distributed Sagas) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 149 Td (* **Expected Depth**: Senior) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 136 Td (* **Key Answer Points**:) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 123 Td (* **Two-Phase Commit \(2PC\)**:) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 110 Td (* Synchronous, blocking protocol coordinating an atomic commit across multiple distributed) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 99 Td (  databases via a Transaction Coordinator.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 86 Td (* **Phase 1 \(Prepare\)**: Coordinator asks all participants to prepare commit and hold) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 75 Td (  locks. Participants vote YES/NO.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8 Tf 0.4 0.4 0.4 rg
45 25 Td (INFOSYS & ENTERPRISE .NET INTERVIEW PREPARATION GUIDE  |  Page 106) Tj ET
