BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 780 Td (* **Correct Implementation**:) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 767 Td (* Wrap the entire processing loop in `ExecuteAsync` with robust `try/catch\(Exception ex\)`) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 756 Td (  blocks.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 743 Td (* Catch exceptions, log structured telemetry with error context, write poison messages to a) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 732 Td (  DLQ, and implement retry backoff without allowing exceptions to bubble out of) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 721 Td (  `ExecuteAsync`.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 708 Td (* **The Trap**: Leaving `ExecuteAsync` unprotected, causing a single malformed message from) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 697 Td (  a queue to crash the entire production web server pod.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 684 Td (* **Follow-up Question**: Can you configure ASP.NET Core to keep the web host running if a) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 673 Td (  background service crashes? *\(Answer: Yes, configure) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 662 Td (  `builder.Services.Configure<HostOptions>\(o => o.BackgroundServiceExceptionBehavior =) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 651 Td (  BackgroundServiceExceptionBehavior.Ignore\)`, though fixing the exception handling is) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 640 Td (  strongly preferred\).*) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 45 627 Td (---) Tj ET
BT /F2 10 Tf 0.2 0.2 0.3 rg 45 611 Td (Q136: Microservice Cascading Failure Post Network Blip) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 596 Td (* **Expected Depth**: Senior) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 583 Td (* **Key Answer Points**:) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 570 Td (* **Scenario**: A 10-second network blip disconnects Service A from Service B. Once the) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 559 Td (  network recovers, instead of recovering, both Service A and Service B crash completely,) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 548 Td (  cascading across the entire architecture.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 535 Td (* **Mechanics of Cascading Failure**:) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 522 Td (* During the outage, requests to Service A back up.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 509 Td (* Service A has aggressive retry policies \(e.g., retry 5 times with no delay or backoff\).) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 496 Td (* When the network reconnects, Service A unleashes a massive **Retry Storm \(Thundering) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 485 Td (  Herd\)** of tens of thousands of retried requests onto Service B.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 472 Td (* Service B is overwhelmed by $10\\times$ normal traffic, CPU hits $100\\%$, thread pools) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 461 Td (  exhaust, and Service B crashes again.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 448 Td (* Service A times out, retries again, and crashes its own memory/thread pool.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 435 Td (* **Architectural Countermeasures**:) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 422 Td (* **Exponential Backoff with Full Jitter**: Randomize retry delays to prevent synchronizing) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 411 Td (  traffic spikes \($T = 2^{\\text{retry}} + \\text{random\\_jitter}$\).) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 398 Td (* **Circuit Breaker \(Polly\)**: Cut traffic to Service B immediately during failure,) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 387 Td (  allowing Service B to recover before testing with trial traffic.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 374 Td (* **Rate Limiting & Shedding Load**: Service B must reject excess traffic fast with HTTP) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 363 Td (  429 / 503 instead of queuing until crash.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 350 Td (* **The Trap**: Implementing fixed retries \(e.g., retry every 500ms\). All failed requests) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 339 Td (  synchronize and hammer the downstream service simultaneously in rhythmic destructive) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 328 Td (  waves.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 315 Td (* **Follow-up Question**: What is "Hedging" in resilience policies, and when is it) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 304 Td (  preferred over Retries? *\(Answer: Hedging sends a duplicate speculative request if the) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 293 Td (  initial request exceeds a p95 latency threshold without waiting for failure, returning) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 282 Td (  whichever completes first to smooth latency outliers\).*) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 45 269 Td (---) Tj ET
BT /F2 10 Tf 0.2 0.2 0.3 rg 45 253 Td (Q137: Docker Container `OOMKilled` \(Exit Code 137\) Diagnosis) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 238 Td (* **Expected Depth**: Senior) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 225 Td (* **Key Answer Points**:) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 212 Td (* **Scenario**: A .NET microservice running in a Kubernetes pod is periodically terminated) Tj ET
