BT /F3 7.5 Tf 0.1 0.15 0.3 rg 52 535 Td (    public class Customer {) Tj ET
BT /F3 7.5 Tf 0.1 0.15 0.3 rg 52 525.5 Td (        public int Id { get; set; }) Tj ET
BT /F3 7.5 Tf 0.1 0.15 0.3 rg 52 516.0 Td (        public string Name { get; set; }) Tj ET
BT /F3 7.5 Tf 0.1 0.15 0.3 rg 52 506.5 Td (        ) Tj ET
BT /F3 7.5 Tf 0.1 0.15 0.3 rg 52 497.0 Td (        public override bool Equals\(object obj\) => obj is Customer c && c.Id == Id;) Tj ET
BT /F3 7.5 Tf 0.1 0.15 0.3 rg 52 487.5 Td (        public override int GetHashCode\(\) => Id.GetHashCode\(\);) Tj ET
BT /F3 7.5 Tf 0.1 0.15 0.3 rg 52 478.0 Td (    }) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 461.5 Td (* **The Trap**: What happens if `Id` is mutated *after* the object is added to a) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 450.5 Td (  `HashSet<Customer>` or `Dictionary<Customer, Order>`?) Tj ET
0.96 0.96 0.97 rg 45 372.5 505 65.0 re f
0.82 0.84 0.88 RG 0.5 w 45 372.5 505 65.0 re s
BT /F3 7.5 Tf 0.1 0.15 0.3 rg 52 428.5 Td (    var set = new HashSet<Customer>\(\);) Tj ET
BT /F3 7.5 Tf 0.1 0.15 0.3 rg 52 419.0 Td (    var cust = new Customer { Id = 1, Name = "Alice" };) Tj ET
BT /F3 7.5 Tf 0.1 0.15 0.3 rg 52 409.5 Td (    set.Add\(cust\);) Tj ET
BT /F3 7.5 Tf 0.1 0.15 0.3 rg 52 400.0 Td (    ) Tj ET
BT /F3 7.5 Tf 0.1 0.15 0.3 rg 52 390.5 Td (    cust.Id = 2; // Mutated after hashing!) Tj ET
BT /F3 7.5 Tf 0.1 0.15 0.3 rg 52 381.0 Td (    Console.WriteLine\(set.Contains\(cust\)\); // Outputs: FALSE!) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 364.5 Td (* **Mechanism**:) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 351.5 Td (* When `set.Add\(cust\)` was called, hash table placed the object into Bucket $H_1$ based on) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 340.5 Td (  `Id = 1`.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 327.5 Td (* Mutating `cust.Id = 2` changes its hash code to $H_2$.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 314.5 Td (* `set.Contains\(cust\)` hashes the object to $H_2$, searches Bucket $H_2$, finds nothing,) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 303.5 Td (  and returns `false`.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 290.5 Td (* The object is now an "invisible ghost" stuck in the hash set, causing memory leaks and) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 279.5 Td (  lookup failures.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 266.5 Td (* **Rule**: Fields used to compute `GetHashCode\(\)` **MUST be immutable** \(`readonly` or) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 255.5 Td (  `init`\).) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 242.5 Td (* **The Trap**: Overriding `Equals` without overriding `GetHashCode\(\)`. Two objects) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 231.5 Td (  evaluated as equal by `Equals` will have different default hash codes, breaking all) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 220.5 Td (  dictionary and set lookups.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 207.5 Td (* **Follow-up Question**: Why is `public override int GetHashCode\(\) => 1;` a terrible) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 196.5 Td (  implementation even though it strictly satisfies the hash code contract? *\(Answer: It) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 185.5 Td (  places all objects into the exact same hash bucket, turning $O\(1\)$ lookups into an $O\(N\)$) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 52 174.5 Td (  linear search\).*) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 0.15 0.15 0.15 rg 45 161.5 Td (---) Tj ET
